{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>One who strikes a man and he dies.</b> Meaning: it is not required that the death occur at the very moment of the blow — as when one slaughters him or burns him or strangles him, thereby extracting his soul from him directly by hand — but even if he dies afterward through the force of the blow, he is liable, unless he recovered from his injury, as it is written (Exodus 21:19): ״אִם יָקוּם״. And the novelty he informs us of is that this is not the law regarding one who kills unwittingly, for he is not obligated to go into exile unless he actually died by his hand, as is taught in the chapter Mi She'ahazo (Gittin 70a): if he slaughtered two simanim or the majority of two simanim, this one does not go into exile, because we are concerned that perhaps the wind displaced it or it was a perikes and hastened his death, etc., end of quote. And the Tosafot wrote that this distinction applies specifically to one who kills unwittingly, for upon this it depends, as it is written ״וַיַּפֵּל עָלָיו וַיָּמֹת״ (Numbers 35:23), which implies immediately, whereas in the case of intentional killing he is liable even if he did not die immediately; there is a distinction between death and exile, end of quote. And these are our words, for we examined precisely the verse's saying in the case of intentional killing <b>one who strikes a man and he dies</b>, and it did not say 'one who kills a man' etc. — to teach that even if he struck him now and he died after several days. But in the case of unwitting killing, it was precise to say <b>that it came by his hand</b> — meaning, that he killed him literally by his hand. And so it follows from the words of the Rambam in chapter 5 of Hilkhot Rotze'ah; but not so is the opinion of the Ra'avad, who does not distinguish — see their words there; and his words do not appear reasonable in any manner whatsoever, and this is not the place for pilpul of the halakhic authorities." "<b>He shall surely be put to death.</b> The reason for the duplication in this law and in the law of one who strikes or curses his parents, and in the kidnapping of a person, and in the striking of one's slave, and the like. Know that the judgments of YHWH — He who judges the entire world — are to put to death the doers of wickedness, except that He chose to give some of them over to earthly judges to put to death those inscribed in the Torah of YHWH, for many reasons, some of which have been revealed to us through contemplation of the Torah. And thus and thus does He judge — the Master above — as in one who lies with a menstruant, and one who desecrates Yom Kippur, and one who eats forbidden fat, and the like: He sends His angel to strike him. And therefore the verse says <b>he shall surely be put to death</b> — meaning, he who is liable to death shall be put to death by your hand, for he is liable to death even if he is not put to death by you. And therefore they said, of blessed memory (Ketubot 30a), even if the judges are unavailable, their judgment is not nullified, and the Judge of the world judges him. And therefore in the law of an ox that gored a person and was warned regarding its owners, it says ״וְגַם בְּעָלָיו יוּמָת״, for there the death is only by the hand of Heaven and it was not given over to the court, as they said, of blessed memory (Mekhilta): ״יוּמָת״ — by the hand of Heaven. And our Sages, of blessed memory (Bava Metzia 31b), said yet another thing on this matter."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:53.896403754Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}